1
00:00:08,065 --> 00:00:13,265
morning, “that you have ordered a good dinner to-day, because I have reason to expect an

2
00:00:13,265 --> 00:00:14,899
addition to our family party.”

3
00:00:14,899 --> 00:00:17,166
“Who do you mean, my dear?

4
00:00:17,166 --> 00:00:21,699
I know of nobody that is coming, I am sure, unless Charlotte Lucas should happen to call

5
00:00:21,699 --> 00:00:24,632
in; and I hope my dinners are good enough for her.

6
00:00:24,632 --> 00:00:27,399
I do not believe she often sees such at home.”

7
00:00:27,399 --> 00:00:32,233
“The person of whom I speak is a gentleman and a stranger.”

8
00:00:32,233 --> 00:00:33,899
Mrs. Bennet’s eyes sparkled.

9
00:00:33,899 --> 00:00:37,033
“A gentleman and a stranger!

10
00:00:37,033 --> 00:00:39,500
It is Mr. Bingley, I am sure.

11
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:40,600
Why, Jane—you never dropped a word of this—you sly thing!

12
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:44,000
Well, I am sure I shall be extremely glad to see Mr. Bingley.

13
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:45,300
But—good Lord!

14
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:46,500
how unlucky!

15
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:48,065
there is not a bit of fish to be got to-day.

16
00:00:48,065 --> 00:00:50,432
Lydia, my love, ring the bell.

17
00:00:50,432 --> 00:00:52,733
I must speak to Hill this moment.”

18
00:00:52,733 --> 00:00:58,565
“It is not Mr. Bingley,” said her husband; “it is a person whom I never saw in the

19
00:00:58,565 --> 00:01:01,132
whole course of my life.”

20
00:01:01,132 --> 00:01:05,533
This roused a general astonishment; and he had the pleasure of being eagerly questioned

21
00:01:05,533 --> 00:01:09,166
by his wife and five daughters at once.

22
00:01:09,166 --> 00:01:14,166
After amusing himself some time with their curiosity, he thus explained:—“About a

23
00:01:14,166 --> 00:01:18,765
month ago I received this letter, and about a fortnight ago I answered it; for I thought

24
00:01:18,765 --> 00:01:22,666
it a case of some delicacy, and requiring early attention.

25
00:01:22,666 --> 00:01:28,199
It is from my cousin, Mr. Collins, who, when I am dead, may turn you all out of this house

26
00:01:28,199 --> 00:01:29,265
as soon as he pleases.”

27
00:01:29,265 --> 00:01:35,332
“Oh, my dear,” cried his wife, “I cannot bear to hear that mentioned.

28
00:01:35,332 --> 00:01:38,399
Pray do not talk of that odious man.

29
00:01:38,399 --> 00:01:41,666
I do think it is the hardest thing in the world, that your estate should be entailed

30
00:01:41,666 --> 00:01:46,432
away from your own children; and I am sure, if I had been you, I should have tried long

31
00:01:46,432 --> 00:01:49,365
ago to do something or other about it.”

32
00:01:49,365 --> 00:01:53,500
Jane and Elizabeth attempted to explain to her the nature of an entail.

33
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:58,199
They had often attempted it before: but it was a subject on which Mrs. Bennet was beyond

34
00:01:58,199 --> 00:02:02,466
the reach of reason; and she continued to rail bitterly against the cruelty of settling

35
00:02:02,466 --> 00:02:07,466
an estate away from a family of five daughters, in favour of a man whom nobody cared anything

36
00:02:07,466 --> 00:02:08,466
about.

37
00:02:08,466 --> 00:02:13,365
“It certainly is a most iniquitous affair,” said Mr. Bennet; “and nothing can clear

38
00:02:13,365 --> 00:02:16,600
Mr. Collins from the guilt of inheriting Longbourn.

39
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:20,265
But if you will listen to his letter, you may, perhaps, be a little softened by his

40
00:02:20,265 --> 00:02:22,432
manner of expressing himself.”

41
00:02:22,432 --> 00:02:27,565
“No, that I am sure I shall not: and I think it was very impertinent of him to write to

42
00:02:27,565 --> 00:02:30,100
you at all, and very hypocritical.

43
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:32,265
I hate such false friends.

44
00:02:32,265 --> 00:02:36,733
Why could not he keep on quarrelling with you, as his father did before him?”

45
00:02:36,733 --> 00:02:42,500
“Why, indeed, he does seem to have had some filial scruples on that head, as you will

46
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:43,500
hear.”

47
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:48,699
“Hunsford, near Westerham, Kent, 15th October.

48
00:02:48,699 --> 00:02:53,432
“Dear Sir, “The disagreement subsisting between yourself

49
00:02:53,432 --> 00:02:58,865
and my late honoured father always gave me much uneasiness; and, since I have had the

50
00:02:58,865 --> 00:03:04,365
misfortune to lose him, I have frequently wished to heal the breach: but, for some time,

51
00:03:04,365 --> 00:03:09,565
I was kept back by my own doubts, fearing lest it might seem disrespectful to his memory

52
00:03:09,565 --> 00:03:15,533
for me to be on good terms with anyone with whom it had always pleased him to be at variance.”—‘There,

53
00:03:15,533 --> 00:03:21,132
Mrs. Bennet.’—“My mind, however, is now made up on the subject; for, having received

54
00:03:21,132 --> 00:03:27,100
ordination at Easter, I have been so fortunate as to be distinguished by the patronage of

55
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:32,800
the Right Honourable Lady Catherine de Bourgh, widow of Sir Lewis de Bourgh, whose bounty

56
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:37,300
and beneficence has preferred me to the valuable rectory of this parish, where it shall be

57
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:42,432
my earnest endeavour to demean myself with grateful respect towards her Ladyship, and

58
00:03:42,432 --> 00:03:48,033
be ever ready to perform those rites and ceremonies which are instituted by the Church of England.

59
00:03:48,033 --> 00:03:53,233
As a clergyman, moreover, I feel it my duty to promote and establish the blessing of peace

60
00:03:53,233 --> 00:03:58,132
in all families within the reach of my influence; and on these grounds I flatter myself that

61
00:03:58,132 --> 00:04:02,365
my present overtures of good-will are highly commendable, and that the circumstance of

62
00:04:02,365 --> 00:04:07,500
my being next in the entail of Longbourn estate will be kindly overlooked on your side, and

63
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:10,800
not lead you to reject the offered olive branch.

64
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:15,500
I cannot be otherwise than concerned at being the means of injuring your amiable daughters,

65
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:20,466
and beg leave to apologize for it, as well as to assure you of my readiness to make them

66
00:04:20,466 --> 00:04:24,233
every possible amends; but of this hereafter.

67
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:28,865
If you should have no objection to receive me into your house, I propose myself the satisfaction

68
00:04:28,865 --> 00:04:33,932
of waiting on you and your family, Monday, November 18th, by four o’clock, and shall

69
00:04:33,932 --> 00:04:38,600
probably trespass on your hospitality till the Saturday se’nnight following, which

70
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:43,800
I can do without any inconvenience, as Lady Catherine is far from objecting to my occasional

71
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:48,332
absence on a Sunday, provided that some other clergyman is engaged to do the duty of the

72
00:04:48,332 --> 00:04:49,332
day.

73
00:04:49,332 --> 00:04:54,565
I remain, dear sir, with respectful compliments to your lady and daughters, your well-wisher

74
00:04:54,565 --> 00:04:56,800
and friend, “William Collins.”

75
00:04:56,800 --> 00:05:03,065
“At four o’clock, therefore, we may expect this peace-making gentleman,” said Mr. Bennet,

76
00:05:03,065 --> 00:05:04,533
as he folded up the letter.

77
00:05:04,533 --> 00:05:09,600
“He seems to be a most conscientious and polite young man, upon my word; and, I doubt

78
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:14,699
not, will prove a valuable acquaintance, especially if Lady Catherine should be so indulgent as

79
00:05:14,699 --> 00:05:15,865
to let him come to us again.”

80
00:05:15,865 --> 00:05:20,966
“There is some sense in what he says about the girls, however; and, if he is disposed

81
00:05:20,966 --> 00:05:24,199
to make them any amends, I shall not be the person to discourage him.”

82
00:05:24,199 --> 00:05:29,399
“Though it is difficult,” said Jane, “to guess in what way he can mean to make us the

83
00:05:29,399 --> 00:05:34,399
atonement he thinks our due, the wish is certainly to his credit.”

84
00:05:34,399 --> 00:05:38,800
Elizabeth was chiefly struck with his extraordinary deference for Lady Catherine, and his kind

85
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:43,899
intention of christening, marrying, and burying his parishioners whenever it were required.

86
00:05:43,899 --> 00:05:47,166
“He must be an oddity, I think,” said she.

87
00:05:47,166 --> 00:05:49,265
“I cannot make him out.

88
00:05:49,265 --> 00:05:52,065
There is something very pompous in his style.

89
00:05:52,065 --> 00:05:55,699
And what can he mean by apologizing for being next in the entail?

90
00:05:55,699 --> 00:05:58,332
We cannot suppose he would help it, if he could.

91
00:05:58,332 --> 00:06:00,832
Can he be a sensible man, sir?”

92
00:06:00,832 --> 00:06:03,466
“No, my dear; I think not.

93
00:06:03,466 --> 00:06:06,899
I have great hopes of finding him quite the reverse.

94
00:06:06,899 --> 00:06:11,932
There is a mixture of servility and self-importance in his letter which promises well.

95
00:06:11,932 --> 00:06:14,565
I am impatient to see him.”

96
00:06:14,565 --> 00:06:19,899
“In point of composition,” said Mary, “his letter does not seem defective.

97
00:06:19,899 --> 00:06:26,000
The idea of the olive branch perhaps is not wholly new, yet I think it is well expressed.”

98
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:31,166
To Catherine and Lydia neither the letter nor its writer were in any degree interesting.

99
00:06:31,166 --> 00:06:35,199
It was next to impossible that their cousin should come in a scarlet coat, and it was

100
00:06:35,199 --> 00:06:39,765
now some weeks since they had received pleasure from the society of a man in any other colour.

101
00:06:39,765 --> 00:06:44,865
As for their mother, Mr. Collins’s letter had done away much of her ill-will, and she

102
00:06:44,865 --> 00:06:49,000
was preparing to see him with a degree of composure which astonished her husband and

103
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,332
daughters.

104
00:06:50,332 --> 00:06:54,666
Mr. Collins was punctual to his time, and was received with great politeness by the

105
00:06:54,666 --> 00:06:56,033
whole family.

106
00:06:56,033 --> 00:07:00,765
Mr. Bennet indeed said little; but the ladies were ready enough to talk, and Mr. Collins

107
00:07:00,765 --> 00:07:04,966
seemed neither in need of encouragement, nor inclined to be silent himself.

108
00:07:04,966 --> 00:07:08,199
He was a tall, heavy-looking young man of five-and-twenty.

109
00:07:08,199 --> 00:07:12,500
His air was grave and stately, and his manners were very formal.

110
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:16,899
He had not been long seated before he complimented Mrs. Bennet on having so fine a family of

111
00:07:16,899 --> 00:07:21,065
daughters, said he had heard much of their beauty, but that, in this instance, fame had

112
00:07:21,065 --> 00:07:25,666
fallen short of the truth; and added, that he did not doubt her seeing them all in due

113
00:07:25,666 --> 00:07:28,100
time well disposed of in marriage.

114
00:07:28,100 --> 00:07:32,132
This gallantry was not much to the taste of some of his hearers; but Mrs. Bennet, who

115
00:07:32,132 --> 00:07:35,265
quarrelled with no compliments, answered most readily,—

116
00:07:35,265 --> 00:07:40,932
“You are very kind, sir, I am sure; and I wish with all my heart it may prove so;

117
00:07:40,932 --> 00:07:42,699
for else they will be destitute enough.

118
00:07:42,699 --> 00:07:44,832
Things are settled so oddly.”

119
00:07:44,832 --> 00:07:49,765
“You allude, perhaps, to the entail of this estate.”

120
00:07:49,765 --> 00:07:52,100
“Ah, sir, I do indeed.

121
00:07:52,100 --> 00:07:56,065
It is a grievous affair to my poor girls, you must confess.

122
00:07:56,065 --> 00:08:00,100
Not that I mean to find fault with you, for such things, I know, are all chance in this

123
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:01,100
world.

124
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:04,800
There is no knowing how estates will go when once they come to be entailed.”

125
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:10,699
“I am very sensible, madam, of the hardship to my fair cousins, and could say much on

126
00:08:10,699 --> 00:08:15,332
the subject, but that I am cautious of appearing forward and precipitate.

127
00:08:15,332 --> 00:08:19,666
But I can assure the young ladies that I come prepared to admire them.

128
00:08:19,666 --> 00:08:25,365
At present I will not say more, but, perhaps, when we are better acquainted——”

129
00:08:25,365 --> 00:08:29,765
He was interrupted by a summons to dinner; and the girls smiled on each other.

130
00:08:29,765 --> 00:08:33,265
They were not the only objects of Mr. Collins’s admiration.

131
00:08:33,265 --> 00:08:38,632
The hall, the dining-room, and all its furniture, were examined and praised; and his commendation

132
00:08:38,632 --> 00:08:42,800
of everything would have touched Mrs. Bennet’s heart, but for the mortifying supposition

133
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:45,666
of his viewing it all as his own future property.

134
00:08:45,666 --> 00:08:50,100
The dinner, too, in its turn, was highly admired; and he begged to know to which of his fair

135
00:08:50,100 --> 00:08:53,399
cousins the excellence of its cookery was owing.

136
00:08:53,399 --> 00:08:57,332
But here he was set right by Mrs. Bennet, who assured him, with some asperity, that

137
00:08:57,332 --> 00:09:01,399
they were very well able to keep a good cook, and that her daughters had nothing to do in

138
00:09:01,399 --> 00:09:02,399
the kitchen.

139
00:09:02,399 --> 00:09:04,666
He begged pardon for having displeased her.

140
00:09:04,666 --> 00:09:09,199
In a softened tone she declared herself not at all offended; but he continued to apologize

141
00:09:09,199 --> 00:09:13,466
for about a quarter of an hour.

142
00:09:13,466 --> 00:09:16,666
CHAPTER XIV

143
00:09:16,666 --> 00:09:25,932
DURING dinner, Mr. Bennet scarcely spoke at all; but when the servants were withdrawn,

144
00:09:25,932 --> 00:09:30,399
he thought it time to have some conversation with his guest, and therefore started a subject

145
00:09:30,399 --> 00:09:35,832
in which he expected him to shine, by observing that he seemed very fortunate in his patroness.

146
00:09:35,832 --> 00:09:40,365
Lady Catherine de Bourgh’s attention to his wishes, and consideration for his comfort,

147
00:09:40,365 --> 00:09:42,300
appeared very remarkable.

148
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:44,600
Mr. Bennet could not have chosen better.

149
00:09:44,600 --> 00:09:47,199
Mr. Collins was eloquent in her praise.

150
00:09:47,199 --> 00:09:51,932
The subject elevated him to more than usual solemnity of manner; and with a most important

151
00:09:51,932 --> 00:09:57,500
aspect he protested that he had never in his life witnessed such behaviour in a person

152
00:09:57,500 --> 00:10:04,832
of rank—such affability and condescension, as he had himself experienced from Lady Catherine.

153
00:10:04,832 --> 00:10:08,932
She had been graciously pleased to approve of both the discourses which he had already

154
00:10:08,932 --> 00:10:11,466
had the honour of preaching before her.

155
00:10:11,466 --> 00:10:16,565
She had also asked him twice to dine at Rosings, and had sent for him only the Saturday before,

156
00:10:16,565 --> 00:10:19,466
to make up her pool of quadrille in the evening.

157
00:10:19,466 --> 00:10:23,699
Lady Catherine was reckoned proud by many people, he knew, but he had never seen anything

158
00:10:23,699 --> 00:10:25,399
but affability in her.

159
00:10:25,399 --> 00:10:30,399
She had always spoken to him as she would to any other gentleman; she made not the smallest

160
00:10:30,399 --> 00:10:34,399
objection to his joining in the society of the neighbourhood, nor to his leaving his

161
00:10:34,399 --> 00:10:37,800
parish occasionally for a week or two to visit his relations.

162
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:42,432
She had even condescended to advise him to marry as soon as he could, provided he chose

163
00:10:42,432 --> 00:10:47,832
with discretion; and had once paid him a visit in his humble parsonage, where she had perfectly

164
00:10:47,832 --> 00:10:53,166
approved all the alterations he had been making, and had even vouchsafed to suggest some herself,—some

165
00:10:53,166 --> 00:10:55,899
shelves in the closets upstairs.

166
00:10:55,899 --> 00:11:01,300
“That is all very proper and civil, I am sure,” said Mrs. Bennet, “and I dare say

167
00:11:01,300 --> 00:11:03,365
she is a very agreeable woman.

168
00:11:03,365 --> 00:11:06,500
It is a pity that great ladies in general are not more like her.

169
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:09,265
Does she live near you, sir?”

170
00:11:09,265 --> 00:11:15,166
“The garden in which stands my humble abode is separated only by a lane from Rosings Park,

171
00:11:15,166 --> 00:11:17,399
her Ladyship’s residence.”

172
00:11:17,399 --> 00:11:21,666
“I think you said she was a widow, sir? has she any family?”

173
00:11:21,666 --> 00:11:27,733
“She has one only daughter, the heiress of Rosings, and of very extensive property.”

174
00:11:27,733 --> 00:11:33,000
“Ah,” cried Mrs. Bennet, shaking her head, “then she is better off than many girls.

175
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,399
And what sort of young lady is she?

176
00:11:35,399 --> 00:11:36,632
Is she handsome?”

177
00:11:36,632 --> 00:11:40,800
“She is a most charming young lady, indeed.

178
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:46,733
Lady Catherine herself says that, in point of true beauty, Miss de Bourgh is far superior

179
00:11:46,733 --> 00:11:51,265
to the handsomest of her sex; because there is that in her features which marks the young

180
00:11:51,265 --> 00:11:53,632
woman of distinguished birth.

181
00:11:53,632 --> 00:11:59,666
She is unfortunately of a sickly constitution, which has prevented her making that progress

182
00:11:59,666 --> 00:12:03,832
in many accomplishments which she could not otherwise have failed of, as I am informed

183
00:12:03,832 --> 00:12:08,199
by the lady who superintended her education, and who still resides with them.

184
00:12:08,199 --> 00:12:14,233
But she is perfectly amiable, and often condescends to drive by my humble abode in her little

185
00:12:14,233 --> 00:12:15,399
phaeton and ponies.”

186
00:12:15,399 --> 00:12:17,699
“Has she been presented?

187
00:12:17,699 --> 00:12:20,033
I do not remember her name among the ladies at court.”

188
00:12:20,033 --> 00:12:25,899
“Her indifferent state of health unhappily prevents her being in town; and by that means,

189
00:12:25,899 --> 00:12:31,199
as I told Lady Catherine myself one day, has deprived the British Court of its brightest

190
00:12:31,199 --> 00:12:32,199
ornament.

191
00:12:32,199 --> 00:12:37,065
Her Ladyship seemed pleased with the idea; and you may imagine that I am happy on every

192
00:12:37,065 --> 00:12:42,233
occasion to offer those little delicate compliments which are always acceptable to ladies.

193
00:12:42,233 --> 00:12:46,832
I have more than once observed to Lady Catherine, that her charming daughter seemed born to

194
00:12:46,832 --> 00:12:52,000
be a duchess; and that the most elevated rank, instead of giving her consequence, would be

195
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
adorned by her.

196
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:59,065
These are the kind of little things which please her Ladyship, and it is a sort of attention

197
00:12:59,065 --> 00:13:02,800
which I conceive myself peculiarly bound to pay.”

198
00:13:02,800 --> 00:13:07,632
“You judge very properly,” said Mr. Bennet; “and it is happy for you that you possess

199
00:13:07,632 --> 00:13:10,365
the talent of flattering with delicacy.

200
00:13:10,365 --> 00:13:15,000
May I ask whether these pleasing attentions proceed from the impulse of the moment, or

201
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,666
are the result of previous study?”

202
00:13:17,666 --> 00:13:23,765
“They arise chiefly from what is passing at the time; and though I sometimes amuse

203
00:13:23,765 --> 00:13:28,565
myself with suggesting and arranging such little elegant compliments as may be adapted

204
00:13:28,565 --> 00:13:35,300
to ordinary occasions, I always wish to give them as unstudied an air as possible.”

205
00:13:35,300 --> 00:13:38,233
Mr. Bennet’s expectations were fully answered.

206
00:13:38,233 --> 00:13:43,132
His cousin was as absurd as he had hoped; and he listened to him with the keenest enjoyment,

207
00:13:43,132 --> 00:13:48,166
maintaining at the same time the most resolute composure of countenance, and, except in an

208
00:13:48,166 --> 00:13:52,733
occasional glance at Elizabeth, requiring no partner in his pleasure.

209
00:13:52,733 --> 00:13:57,000
By tea-time, however, the dose had been enough, and Mr. Bennet was glad to take his guest

210
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:00,765
into the drawing-room again, and when tea was over, glad to invite him

211
00:14:01,765 --> 00:14:02,765
“Protested that he never read novels” H.T Feb 94

212
00:14:02,765 --> 00:14:03,765
to read aloud to the ladies.

213
00:14:03,765 --> 00:14:08,065
Mr. Collins readily assented, and a book was produced; but on beholding it (for everything

214
00:14:08,065 --> 00:14:13,832
announced it to be from a circulating library) he started back, and, begging pardon, protested

215
00:14:13,832 --> 00:14:15,832
that he never read novels.

216
00:14:15,832 --> 00:14:19,565
Kitty stared at him, and Lydia exclaimed.

217
00:14:19,565 --> 00:14:24,265
Other books were produced, and after some deliberation he chose “Fordyce’s Sermons.”

218
00:14:24,265 --> 00:14:29,332
Lydia gaped as he opened the volume; and before he had, with very monotonous solemnity, read

219
00:14:29,332 --> 00:14:34,332
three pages, she interrupted him with,— “Do you know, mamma, that my uncle Philips

220
00:14:34,332 --> 00:14:35,932
talks of turning away Richard?

221
00:14:35,932 --> 00:14:38,399
and if he does, Colonel Forster will hire him.

222
00:14:38,399 --> 00:14:40,765
My aunt told me so herself on Saturday.

223
00:14:40,765 --> 00:14:44,600
I shall walk to Meryton to-morrow to hear more about it, and to ask when Mr. Denny comes

224
00:14:44,600 --> 00:14:46,466
back from town.”

225
00:14:46,466 --> 00:14:51,899
Lydia was bid by her two eldest sisters to hold her tongue; but Mr. Collins, much offended,

226
00:14:51,899 --> 00:14:56,365
laid aside his book, and said,— “I have often observed how little young

227
00:14:56,365 --> 00:15:01,000
ladies are interested by books of a serious stamp, though written solely for their benefit.

228
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:06,432
It amazes me, I confess; for certainly there can be nothing so advantageous to them as

229
00:15:06,432 --> 00:15:07,432
instruction.

230
00:15:07,432 --> 00:15:11,033
But I will no longer importune my young cousin.”

231
00:15:11,033 --> 00:15:16,699
Then, turning to Mr. Bennet, he offered himself as his antagonist at backgammon.

232
00:15:16,699 --> 00:15:21,199
Mr. Bennet accepted the challenge, observing that he acted very wisely in leaving the girls

233
00:15:21,199 --> 00:15:23,466
to their own trifling amusements.

234
00:15:23,466 --> 00:15:28,065
Mrs. Bennet and her daughters apologized most civilly for Lydia’s interruption, and promised

235
00:15:28,065 --> 00:15:33,100
that it should not occur again, if he would resume his book; but Mr. Collins, after assuring

236
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:37,365
them that he bore his young cousin no ill-will, and should never resent her behaviour as any

237
00:15:37,365 --> 00:15:44,233
affront, seated himself at another table with Mr. Bennet, and prepared for backgammon.

238
00:15:44,233 --> 00:15:52,932
CHAPTER XV.

239
00:15:52,932 --> 00:15:53,932
MR.

240
00:15:53,932 --> 00:15:58,132
COLLINS was not a sensible man, and the deficiency of nature had been but little assisted by

241
00:15:58,132 --> 00:16:02,899
education or society; the greatest part of his life having been spent under the guidance

242
00:16:02,899 --> 00:16:08,199
of an illiterate and miserly father; and though he belonged to one of the universities, he

243
00:16:08,199 --> 00:16:13,466
had merely kept the necessary terms without forming at it any useful acquaintance.

244
00:16:13,466 --> 00:16:17,800
The subjection in which his father had brought him up had given him originally great humility

245
00:16:17,800 --> 00:16:24,265
of manner; but it was now a good deal counteracted by the self-conceit of a weak head, living

246
00:16:24,265 --> 00:16:29,199
in retirement, and the consequential feelings of early and unexpected prosperity.

247
00:16:29,199 --> 00:16:33,332
A fortunate chance had recommended him to Lady Catherine de Bourgh when the living of

248
00:16:33,332 --> 00:16:38,199
Hunsford was vacant; and the respect which he felt for her high rank, and his veneration

249
00:16:38,199 --> 00:16:43,000
for her as his patroness, mingling with a very good opinion of himself, of his authority

250
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:48,000
as a clergyman, and his right as a rector, made him altogether a mixture of pride and

251
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:52,199
obsequiousness, self-importance and humility.

252
00:16:52,199 --> 00:16:57,132
Having now a good house and a very sufficient income, he intended to marry; and in seeking

253
00:16:57,132 --> 00:17:02,000
a reconciliation with the Longbourn family he had a wife in view, as he meant to choose

254
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:06,000
one of the daughters, if he found them as handsome and amiable as they were represented

255
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:07,865
by common report.

256
00:17:07,865 --> 00:17:13,166
This was his plan of amends—of atonement—for inheriting their father’s estate; and he

257
00:17:13,166 --> 00:17:18,466
thought it an excellent one, full of eligibility and suitableness, and excessively generous

258
00:17:18,466 --> 00:17:21,533
and disinterested on his own part.

259
00:17:21,533 --> 00:17:24,265
His plan did not vary on seeing them.

260
00:17:24,265 --> 00:17:28,966
Miss Bennet’s lovely face confirmed his views, and established all his strictest notions

261
00:17:28,966 --> 00:17:34,300
of what was due to seniority; and for the first evening she was his settled choice.

262
00:17:34,300 --> 00:17:38,466
The next morning, however, made an alteration; for in a quarter of an hour’s tête-à-tête

263
00:17:38,466 --> 00:17:43,466
with Mrs. Bennet before breakfast, a conversation beginning with his parsonage-house, and leading

264
00:17:43,466 --> 00:17:48,233
naturally to the avowal of his hopes, that a mistress for it might be found at Longbourn,

265
00:17:48,233 --> 00:17:53,300
produced from her, amid very complaisant smiles and general encouragement, a caution against

266
00:17:53,300 --> 00:17:55,332
the very Jane he had fixed on.

267
00:17:55,332 --> 00:18:01,132
“As to her younger daughters, she could not take upon her to say—she could not positively

268
00:18:01,132 --> 00:18:07,699
answer—but she did not know of any prepossession;—her eldest daughter she must just mention—she

269
00:18:07,699 --> 00:18:13,132
felt it incumbent on her to hint, was likely to be very soon engaged.”

270
00:18:13,132 --> 00:18:18,332
Mr. Collins had only to change from Jane to Elizabeth—and it was soon done—done while

271
00:18:18,332 --> 00:18:20,699
Mrs. Bennet was stirring the fire.

272
00:18:20,699 --> 00:18:25,932
Elizabeth, equally next to Jane in birth and beauty, succeeded her of course.

273
00:18:25,932 --> 00:18:30,733
Mrs. Bennet treasured up the hint, and trusted that she might soon have two daughters married;

274
00:18:30,733 --> 00:18:35,632
and the man whom she could not bear to speak of the day before, was now high in her good

275
00:18:35,632 --> 00:18:36,699
graces.

276
00:18:36,699 --> 00:18:41,533
Lydia’s intention of walking to Meryton was not forgotten: every sister except Mary

277
00:18:41,533 --> 00:18:46,699
agreed to go with her; and Mr. Collins was to attend them, at the request of Mr. Bennet,

278
00:18:46,699 --> 00:18:51,365
who was most anxious to get rid of him, and have his library to himself; for thither Mr.

279
00:18:51,365 --> 00:18:56,166
Collins had followed him after breakfast, and there he would continue, nominally engaged

280
00:18:56,166 --> 00:19:01,500
with one of the largest folios in the collection, but really talking to Mr. Bennet, with little

281
00:19:01,500 --> 00:19:04,632
cessation, of his house and garden at Hunsford.

282
00:19:04,632 --> 00:19:07,966
Such doings discomposed Mr. Bennet exceedingly.

283
00:19:07,966 --> 00:19:12,666
In his library he had been always sure of leisure and tranquillity; and though prepared,

284
00:19:12,666 --> 00:19:16,666
as he told Elizabeth, to meet with folly and conceit in every other room in the house,

285
00:19:16,666 --> 00:19:21,765
he was used to be free from them there: his civility, therefore, was most prompt in inviting

286
00:19:21,765 --> 00:19:26,332
Mr. Collins to join his daughters in their walk; and Mr. Collins, being in fact much

287
00:19:26,332 --> 00:19:30,533
better fitted for a walker than a reader, was extremely well pleased to close his large

288
00:19:30,533 --> 00:19:32,699
book, and go.

289
00:19:32,699 --> 00:19:37,733
In pompous nothings on his side, and civil assents on that of his cousins, their time

290
00:19:37,733 --> 00:19:40,132
passed till they entered Meryton.

291
00:19:40,132 --> 00:19:43,865
The attention of the younger ones was then no longer to be gained by him.

292
00:19:43,865 --> 00:19:48,233
Their eyes were immediately wandering up the street in quest of the officers, and nothing

293
00:19:48,233 --> 00:19:53,300
less than a very smart bonnet, indeed, or a really new muslin in a shop window, could

294
00:19:53,300 --> 00:19:55,033
recall them.

295
00:19:55,033 --> 00:20:00,000
But the attention of every lady was soon caught by a young man, whom they had never seen before,

296
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:05,265
of most gentlemanlike appearance, walking with an officer on the other side of the way.

297
00:20:05,265 --> 00:20:10,332
The officer was the very Mr. Denny concerning whose return from London Lydia came to inquire,

298
00:20:10,332 --> 00:20:12,399
and he bowed as they passed.

299
00:20:12,399 --> 00:20:17,300
All were struck with the stranger’s air, all wondered who he could be; and Kitty and

300
00:20:17,300 --> 00:20:22,699
Lydia, determined if possible to find out, led the way across the street, under pretence

301
00:20:22,699 --> 00:20:27,199
of wanting something in an opposite shop, and fortunately had just gained the pavement,

302
00:20:27,199 --> 00:20:31,265
when the two gentlemen, turning back, had reached the same spot.

303
00:20:31,265 --> 00:20:36,300
Mr. Denny addressed them directly, and entreated permission to introduce his friend, Mr. Wickham,

304
00:20:36,300 --> 00:20:40,332
who had returned with him the day before from town, and, he was happy to say, had accepted

305
00:20:40,332 --> 00:20:42,332
a commission in their corps.

306
00:20:42,332 --> 00:20:46,632
This was exactly as it should be; for the young man wanted only regimentals to make

307
00:20:46,632 --> 00:20:48,800
him completely charming.

308
00:20:48,800 --> 00:20:53,699
His appearance was greatly in his favour: he had all the best parts of beauty, a fine

309
00:20:53,699 --> 00:20:56,832
countenance, a good figure, and very pleasing address.

310
00:20:56,832 --> 00:21:01,300
The introduction was followed up on his side by a happy readiness of conversation—a readiness

311
00:21:01,300 --> 00:21:06,666
at the same time perfectly correct and unassuming; and the whole party were still standing and

312
00:21:06,666 --> 00:21:11,332
talking together very agreeably, when the sound of horses drew their notice, and Darcy

313
00:21:11,332 --> 00:21:13,932
and Bingley were seen riding down the street.

314
00:21:13,932 --> 00:21:18,399
On distinguishing the ladies of the group the two gentlemen came directly towards them,

315
00:21:18,399 --> 00:21:20,666
and began the usual civilities.

316
00:21:20,666 --> 00:21:24,666
Bingley was the principal spokesman, and Miss Bennet the principal object.

317
00:21:24,666 --> 00:21:29,132
He was then, he said, on his way to Longbourn on purpose to inquire after her.

318
00:21:29,132 --> 00:21:33,065
Mr. Darcy corroborated it with a bow, and was beginning to determine not to fix his

319
00:21:33,065 --> 00:21:37,399
eyes on Elizabeth, when they were suddenly arrested by the sight of the stranger; and

320
00:21:37,399 --> 00:21:41,332
Elizabeth happening to see the countenance of both as they looked at each other, was

321
00:21:41,332 --> 00:21:43,899
all astonishment at the effect of the meeting.

322
00:21:43,899 --> 00:21:48,765
Both changed colour, one looked white, the other red.

323
00:21:48,765 --> 00:21:52,966
Mr. Wickham, after a few moments, touched his hat—a salutation which Mr. Darcy just

324
00:21:52,966 --> 00:21:55,000
deigned to return.

325
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:56,565
What could be the meaning of it?

326
00:21:56,565 --> 00:22:02,000
It was impossible to imagine; it was impossible not to long to know.

327
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:06,033
In another minute Mr. Bingley, but without seeming to have noticed what passed, took

328
00:22:06,033 --> 00:22:08,600
leave and rode on with his friend.

329
00:22:08,600 --> 00:22:12,166
Mr. Denny and Mr. Wickham walked with the young ladies to the door of Mr. Philips’s

330
00:22:12,166 --> 00:22:15,932
house, and then made their bows, in spite of Miss Lydia’s pressing entreaties that

331
00:22:15,932 --> 00:22:20,600
they would come in, and even in spite of Mrs. Philips’s throwing up the parlour window,

332
00:22:20,600 --> 00:22:23,533
and loudly seconding the invitation.

333
00:22:23,533 --> 00:22:28,765
Mrs. Philips was always glad to see her nieces; and the two eldest, from their recent absence,

334
00:22:28,765 --> 00:22:33,065
were particularly welcome; and she was eagerly expressing her surprise at their sudden return

335
00:22:33,065 --> 00:22:37,132
home, which, as their own carriage had not fetched them, she should have known nothing

336
00:22:37,132 --> 00:22:41,466
about, if she had not happened to see Mr. Jones’s shopboy in the street, who had told

337
00:22:41,466 --> 00:22:45,233
her that they were not to send any more draughts to Netherfield, because the Miss Bennets were

338
00:22:45,233 --> 00:22:51,000
come away, when her civility was claimed towards Mr. Collins by Jane’s introduction of him.

339
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:56,399
She received him with her very best politeness, which he returned with as much more, apologizing

340
00:22:56,399 --> 00:23:01,000
for his intrusion, without any previous acquaintance with her, which he could not help flattering

341
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:05,000
himself, however, might be justified by his relationship to the young ladies who introduced

342
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:06,699
him to her notice.

343
00:23:06,699 --> 00:23:10,865
Mrs. Philips was quite awed by such an excess of good breeding; but her contemplation of

344
00:23:10,865 --> 00:23:16,100
one stranger was soon put an end to by exclamations and inquiries about the other, of whom, however,

345
00:23:16,100 --> 00:23:20,300
she could only tell her nieces what they already knew, that Mr. Denny had brought him from

346
00:23:20,300 --> 00:23:24,699
London, and that he was to have a lieutenant’s commission in the ——shire.

347
00:23:24,699 --> 00:23:29,166
She had been watching him the last hour, she said, as he walked up and down the street,—and

348
00:23:29,166 --> 00:23:34,033
had Mr. Wickham appeared, Kitty and Lydia would certainly have continued the occupation;

349
00:23:34,033 --> 00:23:38,500
but unluckily no one passed the windows now except a few of the officers, who, in comparison

350
00:23:38,500 --> 00:23:42,500
with the stranger, were become “stupid, disagreeable fellows.”

351
00:23:42,500 --> 00:23:46,466
Some of them were to dine with the Philipses the next day, and their aunt promised to make

352
00:23:46,466 --> 00:23:50,733
her husband call on Mr. Wickham, and give him an invitation also, if the family from

353
00:23:50,733 --> 00:23:52,899
Longbourn would come in the evening.

354
00:23:52,899 --> 00:23:57,199
This was agreed to; and Mrs. Philips protested that they would have a nice comfortable noisy

355
00:23:57,199 --> 00:24:01,100
game of lottery tickets, and a little bit of hot supper afterwards.

356
00:24:01,100 --> 00:24:06,365
The prospect of such delights was very cheering, and they parted in mutual good spirits.

357
00:24:06,365 --> 00:24:10,832
Mr. Collins repeated his apologies in quitting the room, and was assured, with unwearying

358
00:24:10,832 --> 00:24:13,632
civility, that they were perfectly needless.

359
00:24:13,632 --> 00:24:17,432
As they walked home, Elizabeth related to Jane what she had seen pass between the two

360
00:24:17,432 --> 00:24:23,033
gentlemen; but though Jane would have defended either or both, had they appeared to be wrong,

361
00:24:23,033 --> 00:24:26,865
she could no more explain such behaviour than her sister.

362
00:24:26,865 --> 00:24:32,365
Mr. Collins on his return highly gratified Mrs. Bennet by admiring Mrs. Philips’s manners

363
00:24:32,365 --> 00:24:33,699
and politeness.

364
00:24:33,699 --> 00:24:38,432
He protested that, except Lady Catherine and her daughter, he had never seen a more elegant

365
00:24:38,432 --> 00:24:43,132
woman; for she had not only received him with the utmost civility, but had even pointedly

366
00:24:43,132 --> 00:24:47,865
included him in her invitation for the next evening, although utterly unknown to her before.

367
00:24:47,865 --> 00:24:52,865
Something, he supposed, might be attributed to his connection with them, but yet he had

368
00:24:52,865 --> 00:25:05,000
never met with so much attention in the whole course of his life.
